            <div xml:id="d126">

                <pb facs="0227" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/505: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-11T13:00:00+00:00">October 11, 1935—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 11—9:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>511. Japanese Chargé d’Affaires called this morning, obviously to check recent
                    Foreign Office statements to him. I discussed with him at length the contents of
                    Department’s 284, October 3, 7 p.m.<anchor n="76" xml:id="d126-f1"/> Commenting
                    on the possible attitude of his Government, he pointed out that if Japan did
                    accept invitation to a conference this year it had already been decided that
                    Ambassador Matsudaira would not return to London in time for him to head the
                    Japanese delegation.</p>
                <p>In discussing the above conversation with Craigie, he laid stress on the fact
                    that while the British were agreeable to qualitative limitation for the long
                    term, and for the sake of reaching an international agreement in present day
                    circumstances, were prepared to accept quantitative limitation even for a short
                    period, the British Government would not favor short term qualitative
                    limitation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
